<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<div class="note"><b class="h">Local claim risks in Los Angeles</b><ul class="chips"><li>Wildfire &amp; smoke</li><li>Santa Ana winds</li><li>Earthquake &amp; fire following</li><li>Storm leaks</li><li>Water losses</li></ul><p class="hoods"><b>Neighborhoods we often work in:</b> Pacific Palisades, Silver Lake, Highland Park, Encino, Westchester, Eagle Rock</p></div>

<h2>Los Angeles claims after January 2025</h2>
<p>The Palisades and Eaton fires of January 2025 destroyed thousands of structures in Pacific Palisades, parts of Malibu and Altadena, pushed by an extreme Santa Ana wind event. Beyond the burn areas, homes across the basin dealt with smoke and ash infiltration and wind damage to roofs, fences and trees. Many of those claims are still being scoped, rebuilt or disputed, and an insurance appraiser in Los Angeles sees every stage of them.</p>
<h2>Neighborhoods and housing</h2>
<p>Los Angeles housing ranges from 1920s Spanish revival and Craftsman homes in Highland Park and Eagle Rock, to mid-century hillside homes in Silver Lake and the Hollywood Hills, to post-war ranch houses in Encino and Westchester. Apartment buildings, including the city’s many 1950s and 1960s “dingbat” buildings, make up a large share of the housing stock. Hillside lots raise rebuild costs through access limits, retaining walls and stepped foundations, and those costs are often under-estimated.</p>
<h2>Earthquakes and fire following</h2>
<p>The 1994 Northridge earthquake caused widespread damage across the San Fernando Valley. Earthquake damage is excluded from standard homeowner policies and covered only by separate earthquake insurance, such as a California Earthquake Authority policy. Fire following an earthquake, however, is generally covered under the standard fire policy, a distinction that matters in a city on several active faults.</p>
<h2>Water and storm losses</h2>
<p>February 2024’s record rainfall caused roof leaks and hillside mudslides across the city. Aging supply lines and slab leaks are a constant source of claims in older homes and apartment buildings, and leaks between units are among the most common condo disputes we see.</p>

<h2>Services available in Los Angeles</h2>
<p>Every California Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Los Angeles and the rest of Los Angeles County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/policyholder-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/home-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/commercial-hoa-claim-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/roof-damage-appraisal/">Roof Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/santa-ana-wind-damage-appraisal/">Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/atmospheric-river-storm-claim-appraisal/">Atmospheric River &amp; Storm Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/water-leak-claim-appraisal/">Water Leak Claim Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Los Angeles property owners</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Do you work on Palisades and Eaton fire claims?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes. We value total-loss rebuilds, smoke and ash claims, and contents for homes affected by both fires, as appraiser or through estimate review.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my Los Angeles homeowner policy cover earthquake damage?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Usually not. Earthquake damage requires separate coverage, such as a California Earthquake Authority policy. Fire following an earthquake is generally covered.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are hillside rebuild costs included in standard estimates?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Often not fully. Access, retaining walls and foundations can add substantially to costs, and we price them separately.</p></div></details></div>
